            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Spanish for the conviction, then the Spanish for the stance."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: argumentar",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "argumentar — \"to argue, to give reasons\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Tienes que argumentar tu respuesta. You have to give reasons for your answer."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "To argue a point with reasons, not to quarrel: a quarrel is discutir or pelearse."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "argumentar is built on argumento, a learned borrowing from Latin argumentum 'proof', from arguere 'to make clear, to prove' (Lewis & Short)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
            }
          ]
